Whether republishing the per-variant grades as open data, with full
attribution, is permitted has not been settled either way: it is not
granted here, and it is not declared blocked. The question is being put to
INALI and the answer is pending. The current status and its date are
recorded in reference/source_terms.csv (permission_status for inali_risk)
and shown on the site's Data page. Until INALI answers, anyone reusing the
risk grades should go to INALI's publication and its terms.

What stays under CC BY 4.0 around it: this project's own work on that
book, which is analysis and not the book's data. That is the join between
the book's entries and the 2008 catalog's variants, risk_grade_aliases.csv,
and the findings on direction conflicts in variant names.

Why the 2008 catalog is treated differently: the files derived from the
"Catálogo de las Lenguas Indígenas Nacionales" (variants.csv,
variant_ipa.csv, variant_localities.csv) remain under CC BY 4.0 as a
compilation. That catalog was published in the Diario Oficial de la
Federación and carries no copyright or rights-reserved notice (none was
found in a search of all 256 pages), which is a different situation from a
book that reserves all rights. The catalog itself still belongs to INALI,
as stated below.

